{"title":"The best movies of 2023 so far (Polygon, 2023-10-07T12:38:36+00:00)","text":"The best movies of 2023 so far (Polygon, 2023-10-07T12:38:36+00:00)
Elizabeth comes with an open mind, ready to inhabit Gracie and fully understand her. And yet, across Todd Haynes’ May December, we see how nothing is as simple as Elizabeth — or the audience — thinks. Gracie is always more than meets the eye: more emotional, more aware, more monstrous. And throughout the movie, Haynes expertly teases out the two women’s mysteries as they each try to craft an identity around the other.

Haynes’ film certainly has callbacks to other, classic movies about selfhood. But in a way, May December feels most in line with something like The Rehearsal, which also has a lot on its mind about how blurry the line between real life and performance can be, and particularly how traumatic it is to ask kids to behave like adults. It’s something Melton’s Joe is tragically caught in the middle of, and something May December always has on its mind, even when it feels like it’s about other things.","entities":["The best movies of 2023 so far","Todd Haynes","May December","Elizabeth","Melton","The Rehearsal","selfhood","real life","performance","Joe","identity","Polygon","trauma","Gracie"],"facts":["The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Stone","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Oscars","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Tom Cruise","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> France","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Tubi"]}

